  "healthTab.title": "Flock health",
  "healthTab.weighAndCheck": "Weigh & check your flock",
  "healthTab.yourBird": "Your bird",
  "healthTab.yourBirds": "Your birds",
  "help.checkAnswer": "It's a quick once-over: how your bird looks, eats, breathes, and acts. Birds tend to hide illness, so small changes you notice over a few days can be the first sign something's off. It's a record you can share with your vet, not a diagnosis.",
  "help.checkQuestion": "What's the daily health check for?",
  "help.close": "Close",
  "help.emailDetail": "We usually reply within a day",
  "help.emailTitle": "Email us",
  "help.householdAnswer": "Household members help every day and keep access until you remove them. Sitters cover a specific trip and only have access during that sit.",
  "help.householdQuestion": "Household members vs. sitters",
  "help.privacyAnswer": "Your birds' information is visible to you, the household members you invite, and anyone you send a sit link to. A sit link works from the day you create the sit until the sit ends, and you can revoke it anytime.",
  "help.privacyQuestion": "Is my information private?",
  "help.quickAnswers": "Quick answers",
  "help.reportDetail": "Tell us what went wrong",
  "help.reportTitle": "Report a problem",
  "help.seeEmergency": "See emergency info →",
  "help.sitsAnswer": "Set up a sit when someone else is caring for your birds. Household members already have access. Anyone else gets a private link by email, no account needed. During the sit they see the day's routine, do a daily health check for each bird, and have your emergency info one tap away. You'll see everything they log.",
  "help.sitsQuestion": "How do sits work?",
  "help.stillStuck": "Still stuck?",
  "help.title": "How can we help?",
  "help.tourDetail": "Two minutes on what Kya & Co. can do",
  "help.tourTitle": "Take the tour",
  "help.urgent": "Worried about your bird right now? Call your avian vet.",
  "help.weighAnswer": "Use a gram scale with a perch or a small container. Weigh at about the same time each day, ideally in the morning before food, and log the number even when it looks the same. Slow changes are easier to see over a few weeks than from one day to the next.",
  "help.weighQuestion": "How do I weigh my bird?",
